\subsection{The overall stability index}{

\deqn{OSt = \frac{RR}{DR}.}

The reasoning behind the ratio is that the two rates measure different halves
of stability. \code{DR} is inverse to homeostasis: a plant that resists change is
pushed down slowly, so a \emph{low} \code{DR} means high resistance. \code{RR} is a direct
measure of resilience: a \emph{high} \code{RR} means a fast return. A stable plant is
therefore one with low \code{DR} and high \code{RR}, and their ratio rises with both.
\code{OSt} is dimensionless, because both rates carry the same
\eqn{[\mathrm{value}]/[\mathrm{time}]} units, which is what makes it
comparable across variables measured on different scales.

